"Education, Youth & Culture Overview & Scrutiny Committee","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-26","committee":"Flintshire County Council","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed; Special Meeting","webcast":false,"url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk/ieListDocuments.aspx?CId=143&MId=6260"},{"date":"2026-11-10","committee":"Environment & Economy Overview & Scrutiny Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-11","committee":"Community & Housing Overview & Scrutiny Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-12","committee":"Corporate Resources Overview & Scrutiny Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Flintshire projects a \u00a37.625m gross overspend at Month 6, down from \u00a38.493m at Month 5","lead":"Flintshire Council is projecting a gross operating deficit of \u00a37.625m on the Council Fund for 2026/27 at Month 6, according to a report to its Corporate Resources Overview and Scrutiny Committee. Using the whole of the remaining \u00a32.704m budget risk reserve would reduce that to a net deficit of \u00a34.921m. The gross figure is \u00a30.868m lower than the \u00a38.493m reported at Month 5, with \u00a31.800m released from deferred ultra-low emission vehicle infrastructure costs and a \u00a30.507m grant set against fleet borrowing costs among the movements.\n\nOut of County Placements are projected at \u00a328.345m against a \u00a325.706m budget, an overspend of \u00a32.639m, even though \u00a32m was added to the budget. The paper says further net placements are \"highly likely\". The rest of Social Services is projected at \u00a3122.275m against \u00a3117.240m, an overspend of \u00a35.035m. Children's professional support teams are \u00a30.625m overspent, largely from agency costs because of \"challenges recruiting and retaining social workers\". The council projects that 82% of its \u00a310.999m of budget reductions will be achieved, leaving a \u00a32.015m shortfall.\n\nUnearmarked reserves are projected at \u00a39.751m after the overspend, down from \u00a318.261m at the start of the year. School reserves stood at a deficit of \u00a34.798m at 31 March 2026, an adverse movement of \u00a34.199m. The paper says school deficits are projected to exceed \u00a38m by 31 March 2027. The 3.5% teachers' pay award from September 2026 compares with the 3.2% in the settlement and the 3.0% the council budgeted. Welsh Government grant covers part of the gap, and the paper says a residual shortfall \"will need to be met from within existing resources\".","iso":"2026-11-12","paper_title":"Enc. 1","paper_url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk/documents/s97795/97795.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Flintshire's contingency reserve falls from \u00a38.272m to \u00a30.748m after one-off costs and a Month 5 forecast","lead":"Flintshire Council's contingency reserve, the money held back for the unexpected, is down to \u00a30.748m from \u00a38.272m, according to a paper before its Cabinet on 13 October 2026. The reserve is the part of its reserves, the money it has put aside, that sits above the base level the council keeps.\n\nThe largest call on it is a projected outturn of \u00a35.789m at Month 5. Outturn is the actual spending figure for a year once it has ended, as against what was budgeted. Here it is a projection part-way through the year. The paper also sets out three one-off pressures the reserve is to fund: \u00a30.142m for children's services legal costs, \u00a30.165m to put in place SharePoint, a document-sharing software system, and \u00a30.036m for temporary posts.\n\nThe four items named in the paper do not by themselves account for the whole fall from \u00a38.272m to \u00a30.748m. The material does not itemise the remainder. The paper's total line reads \"Total Contingency Reserve 0.748\".","iso":"2026-10-13","paper_title":"Enc 4","paper_url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk/documents/s97029/97029.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Flintshire's forecast overspend reaches \u00a38.493m at month five, up \u00a31.403m in a month","lead":"Flintshire County Council expects to overspend its budget by \u00a38.493m this year, according to its latest budget monitoring report (the in-year check of spending against budget) for the Corporate Resources Overview and Scrutiny Committee on 8 October. The figure is \u00a31.403m higher than the \u00a37.090m reported at month four. After the council draws on its budget risk reserve, money set aside for the purpose, the net figure is \u00a35.789m.\n\nSocial services is the largest pressure, with a projected overspend of \u00a35.070m. The paper says costs are based on average service user numbers, which rose by 53 supported people from the previous month, adding \u00a30.344m to home care costs. Out of county placements, where the council pays for children's care and education outside its area, are \u00a32.619m over budget, including \u00a30.764m in children's services and \u00a30.170m in education and youth. That is despite a \u00a32m contingency in the budget. The paper says \"the volatile demand of new placements is exceeding those ended/ending placements.\" Streetscene and transportation also continue to overspend.\n\nSchools' own budgets add a further pressure. Their reserves stood at a combined deficit of \u00a34.798m on 31 March 2026, which is \u00a34.199m worse than a year earlier. Schools are projecting a net deficit of more than \u00a38m by 31 March 2027. The council holds a central budget for school exit costs, and the paper notes a \"significant risk that the approved budget is exceeded\" as schools go through redundancy processes.","iso":"2026-10-08","paper_title":"Enc. 1","paper_url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk/documents/s97164/97164.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-11-17","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Revenue Budget Monitoring Report 2026/27 (Month 6)","paper_url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk/documents/s97688/97688.pdf","headline":"Housing Revenue Account unearmarked balance forecast at \u00a34.492m, 10.31% of total expenditure, against a minimum of 3%","detail":"In-year HRA revenue expenditure is forecast to be \u00a30.075m higher than budget. The Month 6 movements include \u00a31.950m of capital underspend used for void subcontractor costs, offset by a (\u00a32.050m) reduction in capital expenditure from revenue.","category":"reserves","matters":"A HRA reserve well above its prudent minimum gives room for voids and repairs spending, and the reallocation of capital budget to voids is a choice other housing authorities also face.","figures":"\u00a34.492m projected HRA closing balance; 10.31% of total expenditure; 3% minimum; \u00a30.075m in-year overspend; \u00a31.950m void subcontractor costs; (\u00a32.050m) CERA reduction","quote":"which at 10.31% of total expenditure satisfies the prudent approach of ensuring a minimum level of 3%","read_on":"2026-11-13","material":true},{"iso":"2026-11-17","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Revenue Budget Monitoring Report 2026/27 (Month 6)","paper_url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk/documents/s97688/97688.pdf","headline":"Council Tax collection at end of September 2026 at 55.82%, against 56.30% a year earlier","detail":"The paper attributes the 0.48% drop to new regulations governing recovery action and the extended period before the council can take court action.","category":"income","matters":"Collection rates feed directly into a council's cash position and the income it can rely on, and the regulatory cause would be relevant to other councils facing the same change.","figures":"55.82% collection at end of September 2026; 56.30% a year earlier; 0.48% lower","quote":"collections at the end of September being 55.82% compared to 56.30% in the previous year.","read_on":"2026-11-13","material":true},{"iso":"2026-11-17","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Revenue Budget Monitoring Report 2026/27 (Month 6)","paper_url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk/documents/s97688/97688.pdf","headline":"Holiday pay tribunal costs could exceed the \u00a30.254m held in reserves, with over 300 live claims","detail":"The financial impact is still being determined for claims following the Harpur Trust v Brazel decision. The case management orders run to January 2027.","category":"people","matters":"Holiday pay liabilities from the Brazel decision are a common contingent liability across UK councils, and the gap between reserves held and the potential final cost is what a lender or finance director would weigh.","figures":"\u00a30.254m held in reserves; 300+ live employment claims","quote":"There is a risk that the final cost may be considerably more than the \u00a30.254m currently held in reserves for this purpose.","read_on":"2026-11-13","material":false},{"iso":"2026-11-17","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Revenue Budget Monitoring Report 2026/27 (Month 6)","paper_url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk/documents/s97688/97688.pdf","headline":"Medium Term Financial Strategy shows an additional budget requirement of \u00a327.866m for 2027/28","detail":"Cabinet considered the projection in September. Options for meeting the requirement are still being considered, and the Welsh Local Government Provisional Settlement is expected on 1 December.","category":"governance","matters":"A finance director would want to compare the scale of a council's forward budget gap and the timing of the settlement that will determine how it is closed.","figures":"\u00a327.866m additional budget requirement for 2027/28","quote":"showed an additional budget requirement of \u00a327.866m for the financial year 2027/28.","read_on":"2026-11-13","material":true},{"iso":"2026-11-17","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Revenue Budget Monitoring Report 2026/27 (Month 6)","paper_url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk/documents/s97688/97688.pdf","headline":"NJC pay award settled at 3.3% against the 3% budgeted, with \u00a30.334m above budget funded from unearmarked reserves","detail":"The higher-than-budgeted award will also increase the budget requirement for 2027/28. A residual shortfall on the teachers' pay award, after Welsh Government grant, must be met from existing resources.","category":"reserves","matters":"A pay award above budget that is funded from reserves carries into the next year's budget, which is a pressure other councils with the same pay settlement face.","figures":"3.3% NJC award; 3% budgeted; \u00a30.334m excess funded from unearmarked reserves; 3.5% teachers' award; 3.0% budgeted for teachers","quote":"funded from unearmarked reserves in the current financial year.","read_on":"2026-11-13","material":false}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-11-12"},"name":"Flintshire","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-11-13","since_iso":"2026-10-14","distress":false,"headline":"2025/26 capital expenditure includes \u00a380m from a change in lease accounting for the Mynydd Isa Campus Mutual Investment Model scheme, which the paper says is not cash","lead":"Lease accounting changes can make year-on-year capital expenditure look very different from cash spend, and other councils with similar schemes will need to make the same adjustment when comparing figures.","also":["53 other findings in this window","15 papers caught"],"watch":"Cabinet on 2026-11-17","n_findings":54,"n_documents":15,"top_paper_title":"Enc. 2","top_paper_url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk/documents/s97796/97796.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":313,"news_drafts_total":3}}